      "q": "17. The current $I$ flows through a wire of radius $r$ and the free electron drift with a velocity ${{\\upsilon }_{d}}$ what is the drift velocity of electrons through a wire of same material but having double the radius, when a current of $2I$ flows through it?",
      "a": "It is known that,\n$I=neA{{v}_{d}}$\n$\\Rightarrow {{v}_{d}}=\\frac{I}{neA}=\\frac{I}{ne\\pi {{r}^{2}}}$\u00a0 \u2026\u2026$(1)$\nIf ${{v}_{d}}'$ is the drift velocity of electrons in the second wire\n$vd'=\\frac{I'}{nA'e}$\n$\\Rightarrow vd'=\\frac{2I}{n4\\pi {{r}^{2}}e}$\n$\\Rightarrow vd'=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{I}{n\\pi {{r}^{2}}e})$ \u00a0 \u2026\u2026$(2)$\nFrom equations $(1)$ and $(2)$\n$\\Rightarrow vd'=\\frac{vd}{2}$\nTherefore, the drift velocity gets reduced to half."
